/// Stop if a condition is false.
///
/// Checks a result and stops the script with `message` when the check fails: the error is
/// Z0001, the code of `error`, and the exit code is 1 (section 3.8 of the Language Reference).
/// When the check holds, nothing is printed and the script carries on, so an `assert` costs
/// nothing to read and guards a re-run: a row count after a filter, a share between 0 and 1, a
/// coefficient with the expected sign. `assert(equal(f(x), [1, 2]), "...")` is how a project
/// tests its own functions.
///
/// The condition is one Bool. `NA` is neither true nor false and is an error (Z0203), as it is
/// for `if`, so a check on a value that may be missing tests `is_missing` first; a column is not
/// one Bool either (Z0203): reduce it with `all` or `any`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)`.
/// Like R's `stopifnot`, with a message of your own; like Stata's `assert`, which stops with
/// "assertion is false".
///
/// condition: The check, a Bool.
/// message: What to say when it fails.
/// returns: Nothing.
/// missing values: A condition that is `NA` is neither true nor false and stops the script (Z0203): a check on a value that may be missing tests `is_missing` first.
/// common mistake: Giving a comparison of a whole column. That is one answer for each row, not one Bool; check every row with `all`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), "x is positive")`. The message says the same:
/// mistake call: assert(d["x"] > 0, "x is positive")
/// mistake prints: error[Z0203]: `assert` needs one Bool, but this is a Column<Bool>
/// mistake prints:   = help: reduce the column to one Bool: `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)` checks every row, `any` checks for one
/// coming from: assert(n == 3, "three rows expected") | stopifnot("three rows expected" = n == 3) | assert _N == 3
/// coming from: Stata's `assert` checks its condition in every row of the data and has no message of its own; here the condition is one Bool, and `all` makes one of a column. R's `stopifnot` takes several conditions at once; here each has its own `assert` and its own message.
/// related: To stop without a condition, use `error`.
/// related: To compare two values that may be lists or records, use `equal`.
/// see also: error, warn, equal, all
/// example: A check that holds prints nothing, and the script carries on.
/// > assert(1 + 1 == 2, "arithmetic still holds")
/// > let d = frame(x = [1, 2, 3])
/// > assert(d.n_rows() == 3, "three rows expected")
/// > print("both checks held")
/// both checks held
/// example: A check that fails stops the script with its message.
/// > assert(d.n_rows() == 4, "four rows expected")
/// error[Z0001]: four rows expected
///  --> examples.zeff:5:1
///   |
/// 5 | assert(d.n_rows() == 4, "four rows expected")
///   | ^^^^^^ in this call
fn assert(condition: Bool, message: Str) -> Nothing {
    if !condition {
        error(message)
    }
}
